<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Krishnaraja Wodeyar II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1728–1766</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Krishnaraja Wodeyar II (1728–1766) was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> from 1734 to 1766. Throughout his reign real power lay with ministers: first the dalavayi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devaraja">Devaraja</a> and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjaraja">Nanjaraja</a> of the Kalale family, and from 1761 the soldier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a>, who took control of the government. The king remained a figurehead while Mysore was turned into a major military power in his name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/krishnaraja-wodeyar-ii.jpg" alt="Krishnaraja Wodeyar II" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Krishnaraja_Wadiyar_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
